      "id": 1,
      "text": "Oldtown was founded in the 18th century as Opessa's Town, a Shawnee village led by Opessa Straight Tail before 1725.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oldtown%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Thomas Cresap opened a trading post on the abandoned Shawnee village in 1741, marking Oldtown's first European settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oldtown%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Chesapeake and Ohio Canal crossed Oldtown, with Lockhouse 70 overlooking a five‑mile stretch of water toward Town Creek Aqueduct.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.canaltrust.org/discoveryarea/oldtown/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A low‑water toll bridge connects Oldtown to Green Spring, West Virginia, crossing the Potomac’s South Branch.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oldtown%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Western Maryland Railway built a station at Oldtown’s lower end in 1906, linking the town to Cumberland and beyond.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oldtown%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Oldtown’s name evolved from “Shawnee Oldtown” to simply “Oldtown” after the explanatory prefix was dropped.",
